{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>An Egyptian man saved us.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: Now was Moses in the likeness of an Egyptian? Rather, were it not for 'an Egyptian man' — the Egyptian whom he slew — how would he have entered Midian and sat by the well? Three were matched in their match-making by means of the well, and they are Isaac, Jacob, and Moses. Isaac, as is said: ״ויצחק בא מבוא באר לחי רואי״ — 'and Isaac came from the coming of the well of the Living One who sees me' — and immediately after it is written: ״וישא עיניו וירא והנה גמלים באים״ — 'and he lifted his eyes and saw, and behold, camels were coming.' Jacob, as is said (Genesis 29:2): ״וירא והנה באר בשדה״ — 'and he saw, and behold, a well in the field' — and immediately after it is written (ibid.): ״ויהי כאשר ראה יעקב את רחל״ — 'and it came to pass, when Jacob saw Rachel.' Moses, as is said: ״וישב על הבאר״ — 'and he sat by the well' — and immediately after it is written: ״ולכהן מדין שבע בנות״ — 'and the priest of Midian had seven daughters.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:47.546521360Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}